# Allow-list: exclude EVERYTHING, then re-include only what the image needs.
#
# This deliberately fails CLOSED. The previous deny-list version failed open --
# `.claude/` was never listed, so agent notes containing internal hostnames and
# paths were copied into published images by `COPY . .`. Anything not explicitly
# re-included below cannot reach the build context, so a new file added to the
# repo can never leak into an image by default.
#
# The Dockerfile also copies only specific paths, so a leak needs two mistakes.
*

# --- required to build and install the package ---
!pyproject.toml
!README.md
!src/
!environment.yml
# Apache-2.0 §4(a): recipients of the image must get a copy of the License.
# The image also redistributes LGPL-3.0 binaries (XTB, CREST), so shipping the
# license text matters for the OSSAN/attribution work too.
!LICENSE
# Apache-2.0 §4(d) plus the LGPL attribution/source-offer obligations. Both files
# describe THIS image's contents, so they have to travel inside it -- a NOTICE
# that only exists in the git repo does not reach someone who pulls the image.
!NOTICE
!THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md

# Re-exclude junk that could otherwise ride along inside an allowed directory.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.ipynb_checkpoints/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/*.egg-info/

# Belt-and-braces: named explicitly so the intent survives future edits to the
# allow-list above. Later rules win in .dockerignore, so these always apply even
# if someone adds a broad `!` re-include. Every one of these has either leaked
# into a published artefact before, or contains internal paths/credentials.
.git/
.github/
.claude/
.venv/
venv/
dist/
build/
tests/
workflow/
models/
*.sif
*.log
snakemake_report*.html
submit_integration_test.sh
.credentials/
*.pem
*.key
